The '''Dagiti language''' ({{Lang|hil|Sang Dagiti}}; {{Langx|ca|idioma dagití}}) is an [[w:Austronesian languages|Austronesian language]] spoken primarily in the [[Republic of Dagit]] and throughout the [[Shendan Ocean]] region. With approximately 110 million native speakers as of 2024, it is one of the most widely spoken languages in the Fosian-Illypnian maritime sphere and serves as the national language of Dagit, co-official alongside [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]].

Dagiti belongs to the [[Kabisayaan languages|Kabisayaan]] branch of the [[Kabisayunan languages|Kabisayunan]] language family, sharing a common ancestor with the languages of the southern reaches of the archipelago. However, Dagiti has developed distinct phonological and lexical characteristics due to centuries of intensive contact with [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] through the [[Cinc Parets–Macaio galleon trade]].

Unlike most languages of the Kabisayunan family, which were influenced by [[w:Chinese language|Zongese]] through overland trade routes, Dagiti developed its characteristic superstrate vocabulary through maritime commercial contact with Volisanian merchants. This resulted in a unique linguistic profile where Volisanian loanwords dominate commercial, maritime, administrative, and religious domains, while the indigenous Austronesian grammatical structure and domestic vocabulary remain largely intact.

== History ==

=== Proto-Kabisayunan origins ===

Dagiti descends from [[Proto-Kabisayunan language|Proto-Kabisayunan]], a reconstructed proto-language spoken in the central [[Kabisayunan Archipelago]] approximately 1,500 years ago. The proto-language belongs to the [[w:Austronesian languages|Austronesian]] family, which spread across the archipelago through maritime migration beginning around 4,000 years ago.

The separation of Proto-Dagiti from Proto-Kabisayunan occurred during the period of the [[Kingdom of Kabisayaan]] (5th–12th centuries), when the ancestors of the modern Dagiti people migrated northward across the [[Shendan Ocean]] to establish the maritime province of [[Mabatok]] on the northeastern coast of [[Zong]]. This migration introduced the language to a new geographic and cultural environment, where it developed in relative isolation from its southern relatives while absorbing loanwords from the languages of the mainland.

=== The Zongese superstrate (12th–16th centuries) ===

Following the collapse of the Mabatok colony in 1123 and the retreat of the Dagiti to the archipelago, the language entered a period of intensive contact with the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. Zongese merchants established permanent enclaves in the trading ports of the [[Kingdom of Maputik]], introducing vocabulary related to commerce, technology, and governance.

Zongese loanwords from this period include:
* ''pinggan'' (plate) — from Zongese *pingpan*
* ''sutla'' (silk) — from Zongese *sīchóu*
* ''tinda'' (merchandise) — from Zongese *tīandān*
* ''bentahe'' (advantage) — from Zongese *lìyì* (via semantic shift)

This Zongese superstrate remained relatively thin, concentrated primarily in commercial vocabulary, and did not significantly affect the grammatical structure of the language.

=== The Volisanian commercial influence (17th–19th centuries) ===

The most significant transformation of the Dagiti lexicon occurred following the establishment of [[Cinc Parets]] by [[Ernesto Lacunya]] in 1605. Unlike the colonial relationships that shaped other Austronesian languages, the Volisanian presence in Dagit was purely commercial. [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]] became the ''lingua franca'' of the [[Cinc Parets–Macaio Galleon Trade]], and Dagiti merchants adopted Volisanian vocabulary to participate in the lucrative trade network.

The mechanism of Volisanian influence operated through several channels:

* '''Commercial prestige:''' Volisanian was the language of international trade in the Shendan Ocean. Dagiti merchants who learned Volisanian could negotiate directly with Volisanian, Baliscan, and Zongese traders, bypassing intermediaries.
* '''Voluntary education:''' Volisanian-language schools, funded by merchant families and religious institutions, taught literacy and numeracy to the children of the Dagiti elite and the growing ''mestizo'' class.
* '''Religious codification:''' Volisanian friars created orthographies for writing Dagiti using the Volisanian alphabet, codifying the language and introducing Volisanian religious terminology.
* '''Marriage alliances:''' Intermarriage between Volisanian merchants and Dagiti women created bilingual households where children grew up speaking both languages.

By the mid-19th century, an estimated 30–40% of the Dagiti lexicon in commercial, administrative, and religious domains consisted of Volisanian loanwords. However, the core vocabulary—kinship terms, body parts, basic actions, natural phenomena—remained firmly Austronesian.

=== The Revolutionary period and linguistic nationalism ===

The [[Dagiti Revolution of 1850]] and the establishment of the [[Republic of Dagit]] introduced a new dimension to the language's development. The revolutionary movement, led by [[Mateu Malvar]], emphasized the restoration of traditional Dagiti values and identity. This ideological current influenced linguistic attitudes, leading to deliberate efforts to preserve and promote the indigenous elements of the language.

The Constitution of 1850 designated both Dagiti and Volisanian as official languages of the Republic, but the education system established by the revolutionary government prioritized Dagiti literacy for the general population. The ''Komisyon sa Sang Dagiti'' (Commission on the Dagiti Language), founded in 1858, was tasked with standardizing the orthography, producing dictionaries and grammars, and promoting the language in official contexts.

This period also saw the deliberate cultivation of ''indigenist'' vocabulary—coining new Dagiti words for modern concepts rather than simply borrowing from Volisanian. For example, the word for "republic" became ''republika'' (borrowed) but also ''kasugbuan'' (from indigenous roots meaning "agreement" or "covenant"). Such doublets remain common in modern Dagiti, with the borrowed term often used in formal contexts and the indigenous term in nationalist or cultural discourse.

=== Modern Dagiti ===

Contemporary Dagiti continues to evolve as the primary language of everyday communication in the Republic of Dagit. The language maintains its diglossic relationship with Volisanian, with Dagiti serving as the vernacular of daily life and Volisanian retaining prestige in commercial, legal, and international contexts.

The [[w:Mass media|mass media]] of Dagit—television, radio, and print—operate primarily in Dagiti, though Volisanian is used in business news and international programming. The education system teaches both languages from primary school onward, producing a population that is functionally bilingual in both Dagiti and Volisanian.

== Classification ==

Dagiti is classified within the [[Kabisayaan languages|Kabisayaan]] subgroup of the [[Kabisayunan languages|Kabisayunan]] branch of the [[w:Austronesian languages|Austronesian]] language family. Its closest relatives are [[Sugbu]] (spoken in the southern reaches of the Kabisayunan Archipelago) and [[Iraya]] (spoken in the eastern islands).

The genetic relationship between Dagiti and other Kabisayaan languages is close; they share approximately 75% basic vocabulary and are partially mutually intelligible. However, significant differences in phonology and superstrate vocabulary (other Kabisayaan languages influenced by Zongese and other Fosian languages, Dagiti by Volisanian) make full mutual comprehension difficult without exposure.

=== Comparative lexical examples ===

{| class="wikitable sortable"
