The table demonstrates that core vocabulary (water, eat, house) remains cognate across Kabisayaan languages, while commercial and administrative vocabulary diverges due to different superstrate influences—Dagiti from Volisanian, Sugbu from a mix of Zongese and other sources, Iraya from indigenous developments.

== Phonology ==

=== Vowels ===

Dagiti has a five-vowel system typical of Kabisayunan languages:

{| class="wikitable"
! colspan="2" | Front !! Central !! Back
|-
! High
| {{IPAblink|i}} || || {{IPAblink|u}}
|-
! Mid
| {{IPAblink|e}} || || {{IPAblink|o}}
|-
! Low
| || {{IPAblink|a}} ||
|}

All vowels occur in syllable-initial, medial, and final positions. Vowel length is not phonemic.

=== Consonants ===

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! colspan="2" | Labial !! Alveolar !! Palatal !! Velar !! Glottal
|-
! Plosive
| {{IPAblink|p}} {{IPAblink|b}} || {{IPAblink|t}} {{IPAblink|d}} || || {{IPAblink|k}} {{IPAblink|ɡ}} || {{IPAblink|ʔ}}
|-
! Fricative
| || {{IPAblink|s}} || {{IPAblink|ʃ}} || {{IPAblink|x}} || {{IPAblink|h}}
|-
! Affricate
| || || {{IPAblink|tʃ}} {{IPAblink|dʒ}} || ||
|-
! Nasal
| {{IPAblink|m}} || {{IPAblink|n}} || {{IPAblink|ɲ}} || {{IPAblink|ŋ}} ||
|-
! Lateral
| || {{IPAblink|l}} || || ||
|-
! Flap
| || {{IPAblink|ɾ}} || || ||
|-
! Semivowel
| {{IPAblink|w}} || || {{IPAblink|j}} || ||
|}

The phoneme /h/ appears primarily in loanwords (particularly from Volisanian) and in interjections. The phonemes /ʃ/, /tʃ/, /dʒ/, and /x/ occur almost exclusively in Volisanian loanwords, where they have been partially integrated into the native phonology.

=== Stress ===

Primary stress in Dagiti is predictable, falling on the penultimate syllable of the root word. Secondary stress may occur on alternate syllables preceding the primary stress in longer words.

Examples:
* ''kaón'' [kaˈʔon] — "eat"
* ''baláy'' [baˈlaj] — "house"
* ''marsán'' [maɾˈsan] — "merchant" (loanword, stress preserved from Volisanian *marxant*)

=== Phonological adaptations of Volisanian loanwords ===

When Volisanian words are borrowed into Dagiti, they undergo systematic phonological adaptation:

{| class="wikitable"
! Volisanian !! Dagiti !! Process
|-
| ''vaixell'' [bəˈʃeʎ] || ''vaisel'' [bajˈsɛl] || Schwa neutralized to /e/; palatal lateral simplified to /l/
|-
| ''compte'' [ˈkɔmptə] || ''kompt'' [komp] || Final vowel dropped; cluster preserved
|-
| ''preu'' [pɾɛw] || ''preyu'' [ˈpɾɛju] || Semivowel preserved as /j/
|-
| ''magatzem'' [məɣəˈddzɛm] || ''magatzem'' [maɣaˈdzɛm] || Schwa sequences expanded to full vowels
|-
| ''govern'' [ɡuβəɾˈn] || ''gubyern'' [ɡuˈbjɛrn] || Beta approximant adapted to /b/; epenthetic /y/
|}

== Grammar ==

=== Morphology ===

Dagiti is an [[w:Agglutinative language|agglutinative]] language that uses a rich system of affixes (prefixes, infixes, suffixes, and circumfixes) to derive words and indicate grammatical relations. The basic morphological pattern consists of a root to which one or more affixes are attached.

==== Verb morphology ====

Dagiti verbs are marked for [[w:Grammatical voice|voice]], [[w:Grammatical aspect|aspect]], and [[w:Grammatical mood|mood]]. The voice system distinguishes between actor focus, patient focus, locative focus, and instrumental/benefactive focus.

{| class="wikitable"
! Focus !! Affix !! Example !! Meaning
|-
| Actor || ''um-'' || ''um-kaon'' ("eat") || "is eating"
|-
| Patient || ''-on'' || ''kaon-on'' ("eat") || "is being eaten"
|-
| Locative || ''-an'' || ''kaon-an'' ("eat") || "place where something is eaten"
|-
| Instrumental || ''i-'' || ''i-kaon'' ("eat") || "use something to eat"
|}

==== Nominal morphology ====

Nouns are not marked for case or number. Plurality is indicated through the use of the particle ''mga'' (pronounced [maˈŋa]) before the noun, or through reduplication.

Examples:
* ''balay'' — "house" (singular or plural, context-dependent)
* ''mga balay'' — "houses" (plural)
* ''balay-balay'' — "houses" (plural, with distributive connotation)

=== Syntax ===

Dagiti follows a [[w:Verb–object–subject|VOS]] (verb–object–subject) word order in basic clauses, though other orders are possible depending on focus and pragmatic factors. The language uses a focus system where the noun phrase that is the topic of the sentence (the "focus") is marked with the particle ''ang'', while other noun phrases are marked with ''sang'' (definite) or ''sing'' (indefinite).

Example sentence:
:''Nag-kaon sang isda ang bata.''
:Ate-[[w:Perfective aspect|PFV]] fish [[w:Definite article|DEF]] child
:"The child ate fish."

Analysis:
* ''Nag-kaon'' — verb (actor focus, perfective aspect)
* ''sang isda'' — object (definite marker + noun)
* ''ang bata'' — subject/focus (focus marker + noun)

=== Pronouns ===

Dagiti pronouns distinguish three persons and two numbers. Special forms exist for inclusive and exclusive first-person plural.

{| class="wikitable"
! !! Singular !! Plural
|-
! First person (exclusive)
| ''ako'' || ''kami''
|-
! First person (inclusive)
| — || ''kita''
|-
! Second person
| ''ikaw'' || ''kamo''
|-
! Third person
| ''siya'' || ''sila''
|}

== Vocabulary ==

=== Lexical structure ===

The Dagiti lexicon is stratified into three layers:

* '''Indigenous layer:''' Approximately 60% of the core vocabulary, consisting of inherited Austronesian roots. This layer includes kinship terms, body parts, basic actions, natural phenomena, and cultural concepts.
* '''Volisanian layer:''' Approximately 25–30% of the total vocabulary, concentrated in commercial, maritime, administrative, religious, and technological domains.
* '''Zongese layer:''' Approximately 5–10% of the vocabulary, primarily in commercial and luxury goods categories.

