== Writing system ==

Dagiti is written using a modified [[w:Latin script|Latin alphabet]] derived from the Volisanian orthography established by Volisanian friars in the 17th century. The alphabet consists of 28 letters:

{| class="wikitable"
|+ Dagiti alphabet
! A || B || C || D || E || F || G || H || I || J || K || L || M || N || Ñ || Ng || O || P || Q || R || S || T || U || V || W || X || Y || Z
|}

The letters ''C'', ''F'', ''J'', ''Ñ'', ''Q'', ''V'', ''X'', and ''Z'' occur primarily in loanwords from Volisanian and other languages. The digraph ''Ng'' is treated as a single letter in the traditional ordering.

=== Orthographic conventions ===

The orthography follows a largely phonemic principle, with each letter representing a single phoneme. Notable conventions include:

* ''Ng'' represents the velar nasal /ŋ/
* ''Ny'' represents the palatal nasal /ɲ/ (in Volisanian loanwords)
* ''Ty'' and ''Dy'' represent the palatal affricates /tʃ/ and /dʒ/ (in Volisanian loanwords)
* ''Si'' before a vowel represents /ʃ/ (in Volisanian loanwords)
* The glottal stop /ʔ/ is indicated by a hyphen between vowels or is unwritten word-initially

=== Historical orthography ===

Prior to the Volisanian codification, Dagiti was written using a variety of indigenous scripts derived from the ancient ''Surat Kabisayunan'' writing traditions of the southern reaches of the archipelago. These scripts, collectively known as ''Surat Dagiti'' (Dagiti writing), were used primarily for religious and ceremonial purposes rather than for commercial or administrative documentation.

The Volisanian friars who codified the language in the 17th century chose to use the Latin script rather than the indigenous scripts, arguing that the Latin alphabet was better suited to representing the Volisanian loanwords that had entered the language. This decision permanently established the Latin script as the primary writing system for Dagiti.

== Dialects ==

Dagiti dialects can be grouped into three major regional varieties:

=== Central Dagiti (Akyatan) ===

The prestige dialect of the language, spoken in the capital region of [[Akyatan]] and the surrounding central islands. This dialect serves as the basis for the standard written language and is the variety used in education, media, and official communications. Central Dagiti has the highest concentration of Volisanian loanwords, reflecting the historical role of Akyatan (formerly Cinc Parets) as the center of the galleon trade.

=== Northern Dagiti ===

Spoken in the northern islands of the archipelago, this dialect retains more Zongese loanwords and has preserved certain archaic phonological features lost in the central dialect. Northern Dagiti speakers often maintain a distinction between native /r/ and the tapped /ɾ/ of Volisanian loanwords, a distinction that has been neutralized in the central dialect.

=== Southern Dagiti ===

The southern dialect, spoken in the islands closest to [[Yanbaru]] and the southern Kabisayunan Archipelago, shows significant influence from [[Yanbarun language|Yanbarun]] and other southern Kabisayunan languages. This dialect has fewer Volisanian loanwords than the central dialect and preserves more indigenous vocabulary. Contact with southern languages has also introduced certain phonological features, such as the retention of syllable-final consonants in some contexts.

== Status ==

=== Official status ===

Dagiti is designated as the national language of the [[Republic of Dagit]] by the Constitution of 1850, and serves as co-official language alongside [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]]. The Constitution specifies that Dagiti is the "national language of the Dagiti people" and requires its use in primary education, while Volisanian is designated as the "language of international commerce" and is required in secondary and higher education.

The ''Komisyon sa Sang Dagiti'' (Commission on the Dagiti Language), a government agency established in 1858, is responsible for regulating the language, developing educational materials, and advising the government on linguistic matters.

=== Education ===

The Dagiti educational system operates on a bilingual model. Primary education (grades 1–6) is conducted primarily in Dagiti, with Volisanian taught as a second language beginning in grade 3. Secondary education (grades 7–12) uses both languages, with Dagiti dominating in humanities and social sciences and Volisanian in mathematics, sciences, and commerce. Higher education operates primarily in Volisanian, particularly in professional fields such as law, medicine, and engineering.

=== Media ===

Dagiti-language media is extensive in Dagit. Major newspapers, including ''Ang Balita sang Dagit'' (The News of Dagit) and ''Ang Timawa'', publish primarily in Dagiti with Volisanian sections for international news. Television and radio broadcasting is predominantly in Dagiti, though Volisanian-language programming exists for business news and international sports coverage.

=== Diaspora ===

Significant Dagiti-speaking communities exist in [[Balisca]] (particularly in the [[Macaio]] region), [[Okaiken]], [[Yanbaru]], and [[Zong]]. These diaspora communities maintain the language through community schools, media consumption, and regular contact with the homeland. The [[Barrio Dagiti]] in Macaio, established during the galleon trade era, remains an important cultural center for the Dagiti diaspora.

